.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_FMT 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_fmt_writer
.Nd formatted output to a writer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fmt.h
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_fmt_writer "t_writer *writer" "t_buffer fmt" "t_u64 *values"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn xft_fmt_writer
writes
.Fa fmt
to
.Fa writer ,
replacing each conversion with the text for the next value taken from
.Fa values .
.Fa fmt
is a length-delimited buffer and need not be NUL-terminated.
Arguments are not variadic: every value occupies one or two
.Vt t_u64
slots of
.Fa values ,
consumed in order.
.Pp
Literal text and each converted value are passed to
.Fn xft_writer_write
separately, so they are staged in the writer and only reach the destination
when its buffer drains or
.Fn xft_writer_flush
is called.
.Pp
A conversion is a
.Ql %
followed by one character.
There are no flags, field widths, precisions or length modifiers.
Decimal output has no padding and no
.Ql +
sign; hexadecimal output has no
.Ql 0x
prefix.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm %b , %w , %u , %q
Unsigned decimal of the low 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits of the slot.
.It Cm %y , %o , %d , %i
Signed decimal of the low 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits of the slot, sign-extended.
.It Cm %X
Uppercase hexadecimal of the whole slot.
.It Cm %x , %p
Lowercase hexadecimal of the whole slot; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It Cm %f
The slot bits reinterpreted as a
.Vt t_f64 .
Printed as an optional
.Ql - ,
the integer part, a
.Ql \&.
and exactly six fraction digits, truncated rather than rounded.
NaN prints
.Ql nan
and either infinity prints
.Ql inf ,
with no sign.
Values of at least 2^64 print
.Ql ovf .
Negative zero prints
.Ql 0.000000 .
.It Cm %s
Two slots: a pointer, then a byte count.
That many bytes are copied verbatim; NUL is not special.
.It Cm %%
A literal
.Ql % .
Consumes no slot.
.El
.Pp
Any other character after
.Ql % ,
including
.Cm %e
and
.Cm %m
which are listed in a comment in
.In fmt.h ,
is an error.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_fmt_writer
returns
.Dv OK
if every write succeeded.
It returns
.Dv KO
if a write to
.Fa writer
fails or an unknown conversion is found.
Output produced before the failure has already been passed to
.Fa writer .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

void	print_particle(t_writer *w, t_u64 id, t_i32 x, t_i32 y)
{
	static const char	fmt[] = "particle %q at (%d, %d)\en";
	t_u64				values[3];

	values[0] = id;
	values[1] = (t_u32)x;
	values[2] = (t_u32)y;
	xft_fmt_writer(w, xft_fatptr((t_u8 *)fmt, sizeof(fmt) - 1), values);
}

void	print_ratio(t_writer *w, t_f64 r)
{
	static const char	fmt[] = "%s = %f\en";
	static const char	name[] = "ratio";
	t_u64				values[3];

	values[0] = (t_uptr)name;
	values[1] = sizeof(name) - 1;
	values[2] = (t_dp){.f = r}.i;
	xft_fmt_writer(w, xft_fatptr((t_u8 *)fmt, sizeof(fmt) - 1), values);
}
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Fa r
equal to 0.5,
.Fn print_ratio
writes
.Ql ratio = 0.500000 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_io 3 ,
.Xr xft_math 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fa values
must not be
.Dv nullptr
even when
.Fa fmt
has no conversions, and
.Fa fmt Ns .mem
must not be
.Dv nullptr .
The number of slots is not checked against
.Fa fmt .
.Pp
.Cm %x
and
.Cm %p
are broken: each nonzero digit is ORed with 1 instead of being lowercased, so
letters stay uppercase and even digits print as the next odd one
(0xabcdef prints
.Ql ACCEEG ) .
A zero value prints
.Ql 0
correctly.
.Pp
A lone
.Ql %
at the very end of
.Fa fmt
writes the literal text before it twice, then the
.Ql % .
.Pp
Negative values at or below \-2^64 are not caught by the
.Ql ovf
check and convert with undefined behavior.
